From ae56c7ccca76f5a7afa1687aefac234935189d97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 03:46:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 56/57] clamp(), the dynamic viewport units,
 prefers-color-scheme, rem in SVG

Four things pages now reach for constantly, each cheap because the engine
already had most of what they need:

clamp(a, b, c) is parsed as max(a, min(b, c)), which is what it is defined to
be, so it lands on the min() and max() support that is already there. A
declaration using clamp() was being dropped entirely, which is how fluid type
and spacing collapse to the initial value.

dvh, svh, lvh and their width counterparts are the viewport units this window
already has: a desktop browser has no toolbar that comes and goes, so the
large, small and dynamic viewports are the same viewport, and aliasing them is
the correct answer rather than an approximation.

prefers-color-scheme answers "light", which these systems always are. It has
to answer at all: a stylesheet that puts its ordinary colours inside a
(prefers-color-scheme: light) block loses them otherwise. Note a media feature
needs registering in three places - the name list, the evaluator, and both
validity lists in MediaQueryExpression - or the query parses into nothing and
the rule is silently dropped.

SVG geometry attributes accept rem, as SVG 2 says they may. Icons sized
width="0.75rem" were coming out at zero, which is a large share of the missing
icons on amazon.com.
